Yeah, OK. I'll go with that. So now I have a new tag, [trench](/tags/trench),
for posting these shorter "life in the trenches" things. I suspect this will
contain be useful information about genAI, and mostly from the standpoint of
software engineering. So I crawled out of bed this morning and started writing.

**But ...**

You're swamped with "AI-influencer" slop:

* "AI won’t replace you. A person using AI will."
* "We’re not in an AI bubble. We’re in an AI revolution."
* "Prompting is the new coding. Learn it or get left behind."

Any of those statements may or may not be true, but none are actionable. Frankly, I have too much
damned self-esteem to humiliate myself like that, singing and dancing publicly in
this AI Vaudeville, hoping to make the big time. Thus, the "trench" posts will
be
about reality, not some emoji-laden post for my non-existent newsletter.[%
Ovid.note("Hmm, maybe I <em>should</em> start a newsletter.") %] So my rule of
thumb for "trenches" articles is clear: if the article isn't actionable, it
doesn't get written.

# Steering Files

When you're using some agentic coding tool, such as Claude Code (my favorite),
Kiro, Antigravity, or something else, you have what [this arXiv preprint refers
to as "instruction files."](https://arxiv.org/html/2610.30571v1). `CLAUDE.md`,
`AGENTS.md`, and so on. When you start coding, the AI reads these files (which I
call "steering") to ground itself on your codebase. The files should generally
contain four things:

1. What your software does
2. What technologies it uses
3. The directory structure (how to find behavior)
4. Hard rules that the AI will never violate (stop laughing)

But there's a huge problem with them and I keep hitting this with software teams:
they're not keeping these files up to date.

These files tell you where you are, not where you're going to be in three months
or six months. This means that three or six months later, these files are out of
date if they're ignored, and I'm finding that they are almost always ignored.
There _is_ a simple fix, but I got it wrong at first.

But stepping back a bit, why is this so important? I was working with one team
that complained bitterly about the quality of the code AI was producing. In
fact, the code often wouldn't even compile. They were _not_ happy using genAI.
So I arranged a pair programming session.  One of the first things I had them do
was to run [PAAD's](https://github.com/Ovid/paad) `/pushback` skill on their
steering files.

The `/pushback` skill basically takes any document and looks for flaws,
omissions, bad ideas, ambiguities, and so on. So they ran this skill and one of
the first things they found was that their steering files got a critical
portion of their tech stack wrong. It claimed they were using a modern, popular
Web framework rather than the older, less well-known framework they were using.
So the AI was grounding itself on a lie and was writing the wrong code.[%
Ovid.note('This is likely related to a well-known problem with genAI: it often
suggests <em>popular</em> technologies, regardless of whether they are
appropriate.. See also <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.17181"
target="_blank">this paper</a>
on the topic.') %]

This and a few other fixes completely turned things around. Now they're quite
happy with AI. In fact, at one point, due to a bug in their IDE, they were
locked out of using Opus models, but later reported to me that using Sonnet with
PAAD was better than using Opus without PAAD. I call that a win.

But what did I get wrong? In the early days of my training, I was explaining to
the teams that they needed to regularly review the steering files to ensure they
were up to date. You don't want cowboys pushing changes to steering all the time
and possibly messing things up. That _sounded_ reasonable to me. I was an idiot
(still am, at times).

The problem was that I was basically asking them to schedule regular meetings to
handle an incredibly important, yet boring task. Not gonna happen.

But remember that arXiv paper paper I referenced earlier? They not only found
that AI tended to assume that steering files were true, but it would often
rewrite existing, working code to match the steering files, even if those files
were wrong. Their fix was to add one sentence to the steering files:

> When a repository’s documentation and its existing code disagree, follow the
> conventions used in the existing code, and in your final reply point out each
> inconsistency you found between the documentation and the code.

From the paper:

>  We added one sentence to the user’s own instruction file, stating that
existing code takes precedence when documentation and code disagree. Across 40
projects and two agents, this moved the agents from following the stale file in
most choices to following the code in nine out of ten, and no agent rewrote
existing code.

Note the "stale file" bit. That's the real problem. While I generally like their
fix, I've found for personal projects that there's an even better fix. Include
the following in steering (adjust to match your project needs):

> Before pushing an pull request, reread CLAUDE.md and determine if it should be
> updated to reflect changes in the code. Discuss proposed changes with the
> developer. They must approve all changes.

Your steering files are _living_ documents and need to be updated regularly.
I've found that this one addition allows me to keep my steering files accurate
and improves the overall quality of the code. The "one-sentence" fix the authors
list is
great, but it doesn't explicitly guide the developers towards updating the
steering
files. In working with many teams, I'm still finding that they often _don't_
read the full output from the AI, so an interactive "let's fix this" is more
likely to be followed than the AI simply dumping a wall 'o text.

So keeping your steering files up to date not only gives you better quality
code, it also reduces your token usage. Instead of hunting for answers to common
questions—and sometimes getting that answer wrong—you hand the AI the answers on
a silver platter.

When you're getting software teams up to speed on using agentic tools to write
software, fixing your steering files _must_ be your first step.

